#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268ee3f5-ff62-526c-bf07-b0aae6059055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269608} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e320c7-d04c-5cac-8eca-b6a622015283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270744} {:answer/id #uuid "a762e70a-2bf7-5828-83b6-2879ecead2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270767} {:answer/id #uuid "e23a2964-03f4-55d3-83a8-a9094a21cbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271257} {:answer/id #uuid "e42fb063-1a4d-51c6-9072-fbbe5660f809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "268ee3f5-ff62-526c-bf07-b0aae6059055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269608} :answer-field/id #uuid "2972b933-504c-5f0a-b4df-b81a8c22a6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a029be5-b6a5-506a-8947-8d15614953ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-140537" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an  orthonormal basis of $V.$   \n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ and   \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}|+|\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\,|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a9e964-e971-5a63-8ddf-d35611c16d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323997} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6569f2-157f-59bd-b487-d77abba9f8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324450} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee6004c-940f-594a-9082-a66d246e0808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324618} {:answer/id #uuid "7e307e2d-eb04-5931-a49e-da669978c621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325033} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a72c2b-2e25-5ac5-9983-7b64eb42b19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ee6004c-940f-594a-9082-a66d246e0808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324618} :answer-field/id #uuid "67bfe10d-3e1e-5ac4-80e9-14d87bdcdeaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fc90254-ed2d-508a-b704-d310ff8d6596" :question/math-academy-id "q-40741" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=\\frac{3}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72cc1442-df79-5623-bff4-42eedddafdb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414732} {:answer/id #uuid "77e7d035-74cb-5a21-8a35-1b0d4898e245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414733} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa54ac6-f50b-5137-953c-f00902b1d420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414738} {:answer/id #uuid "be937ae3-0668-5fe5-9548-61b224487acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414741} {:answer/id #uuid "c38c74ea-a21b-5c0e-bb44-84641a158c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72cc1442-df79-5623-bff4-42eedddafdb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414732} :answer-field/id #uuid "969beab5-314b-57a6-99da-182f476a0e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0934e8ac-174d-5792-a7fc-4ab5bbca2aae" :question/math-academy-id "q-41971" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05279c88-25ab-5a54-aa56-e1c60f9aa5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348493} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd86a7e-ebb1-5da0-bac2-0d8d9bae3547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348722} {:answer/id #uuid "751c6092-53d7-5c48-bc24-abed2d5cb212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349522} {:answer/id #uuid "90b55236-9748-5bf7-bc28-e5120721cb76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349747} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f2495a-31fd-513b-b278-24a29310f2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7f2495a-31fd-513b-b278-24a29310f2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350075} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b31e20-02a7-56ce-9f42-143b07559366" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46809f69-3556-5ede-b407-68133439141e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81211" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=6,$ $c=5,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f02408